---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import PromptList from '../components/PromptList.astro';
import prompts from '../data/prompts.json';

const count = prompts.filter((p) => p.tool === 'v0').length;
---

<Layout
  title={`v0 Prompts: ${count}+ Copy-Ready UI Generation Prompts (2026) | VibePrompt`}
  description="Copy-ready v0 prompts: shadcn-style SaaS landing pages, analytics dashboards, pricing pages with toggles and waitlist forms with validation. Paste into v0 and get a Next.js + Tailwind page."
>
  <nav class="text-sm text-muted-foreground mb-4"><a href="/" class="hover:text-foreground">Home</a> / v0 Prompts</nav>
  <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-bold mb-4">v0 Prompts</h1>
  <p class="text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed mb-4 max-w-3xl">
    v0 (by Vercel) generates React + Tailwind + shadcn/ui from a description — so the quality of what you get back depends almost entirely on
    how precisely you name the sections, components and states. The prompts below speak v0's native vocabulary: sticky navs, tier cards with
    "Most popular" badges, collapsible sidebars, range toggles, success states. Name it exactly and v0 nails it; leave it vague and you'll get a generic page.
  </p>
  <p class="text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl">
    Two habits make v0 output production-usable: always specify <strong>real copy instead of lorem ipsum</strong>, and always describe the
    <strong>states</strong> (loading, error, success) — not just the happy path.
  </p>
  <PromptList filter={(p) => p.tool === 'v0'} />
</Layout>
